{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793573044,"nonce":"yfszX4cucd/Z5j6KXbKg0susu5g=","message":"Refinement from review: promote Asm functions instead of only failing loudly.\n\nCurrent state, read from the code: the hybrid promotion gate (ash_interp interpreter/mod.rs, check on jit_unsupported) refuses any function containing Asm or Prefetch with 'unsupported_opcode', so such a function only ever runs in the interpreter, where Asm is a no-op. --mode jit (compiled_only) skips that gate. The LLVM lowering (llvm/function/memory.rs emit_air_asm) already emits mode 0 as a '.byte' inline asm, but treats modes 1-3 (CPU register scratch / VM register to CPU register / CPU register to VM register) as no-ops and emits each byte as a separate asm statement, so the bytes never see the VM values they were written for. Cranelift has no way to embed raw bytes.\n\nPlan, x86-64 hosts: (1) lower a whole fragment as ONE inline asm block: the bytes as the template, mode-2 registers as pinned inputs ({rax} etc. by HashLink's register numbering), mode-3 registers as pinned outputs, mode-1 as clobbers, plus memory; (2) make the gate treat Asm functions as must-compile on the LLVM tier, compiled synchronously on first call so the interpreter never runs one; this gives exactly HashLink's semantics because the same bytes execute natively. Mode 4 (naked) stays unsupported. (3) Everywhere the bytes cannot run (arm64, wasm, --mode interp) fail loudly naming the function, instead of the silent no-op. (4) The AIR lifter stays the portable follow-up if a program needs it off x86-64.","files":null}]}